Osteoarthritis (Hip or Knee or Generalised)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Mechanical joint pain, Brief morning stiffness, Bony crepitus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-037
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of knee pain with profound stiffness and inability to weight-bear following prolonged standing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an elderly patient with known joint disease who experienced a sudden, severe flare of knee pain and a "locked" sensation, rendering them unable to walk from the living room to the bedroom. Urgent assessment was strictly required to differentiate an acute mechanical osteoarthritis flare from a septic joint or acute meniscal tear, and to provide immediate potent analgesia and mechanical support to enable safe mobilization prior to daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My knee has completely seized up; it feels like bone grinding on bone, and the pain is so sharp I can't put any weight on it to get to bed."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of chronic right knee osteoarthritis. Timeline: Insidious onset of deep, poorly localized right knee ache over several years, known to worsen with prolonged weight-bearing. Today, after spending four hours standing at a family event, the patient developed severe, escalating mechanical joint pain. The patient currently reports extreme "gel phenomenon," characterizing the joint as completely stiffened and locked after sitting on the sofa for the past hour. Any attempt to stand produces agonizing pain and a feeling of giving way. Reports brief morning stiffness historically lasting <30 mins. Denies any recent trauma, fevers, or localized redness.
Functional Status: Acutely immobilized on the sofa; unable to ambulate even with standard walking frame due to severe pain inhibition.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk; entirely dependent on spouse for transfers.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 152/90 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 92 Kg
General: Alert, in visible distress when attempting to move the right leg.
Physical Exam: Right knee reveals palpable, hard, bony enlargement around the joint margins (osteophyte formation), predominantly on the medial aspect. A mild, cool, non-inflammatory joint effusion is present. The joint is not hot or red. Exquisite tenderness is elicited along the medial joint line upon deep palpation. Coarse, prominent crepitus is audible and palpable throughout the active and passive arcs of motion. Restricted joint range of motion noted, specifically lacking the terminal 10 degrees of full extension due to a mechanical, bony block. Ligamentous testing (varus/valgus, Lachman) is stable but painful. The contralateral knee demonstrates similar but less severe osteoarthritic changes.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s sleeping quarters are on the same level, but require navigating a narrow hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: Took regular Paracetamol 1g and Meloxicam 15mg today; completely inadequate for this acute overload flare.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute mechanical overload flare of severe Knee Osteoarthritis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out clinically by the absence of systemic fever, lack of intense joint heat/erythema, and the clear mechanical trigger (prolonged standing) preceding the flare.

Acute Meniscal Tear - Difficult to rule out entirely without MRI, but the classic chronic history, bony enlargement, and coarse crepitus strongly suggest the primary driver is end-stage degenerative change rather than an isolated new soft tissue tear.

Gout (Pseudogout/CPPD) - Ruled out by the lack of hyperacute onset with intense inflammation (red, hot, swollen joint), though CPPD can coexist with OA.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, immobilizing acute-on-chronic flare of advanced knee osteoarthritis, precipitated by a significant mechanical overload event. The critical clinical task was to firmly exclude a septic joint or a catastrophic structural failure. Clinical examination confirmed the presence of classic advanced osteoarthritic signs (bony enlargement, coarse crepitus, cool effusion, mechanical block) without systemic or localized inflammatory red flags. The severity of the pain and the profound "gel phenomenon" stiffness left the patient functionally stranded. Postponing evaluation would have resulted in unmanaged severe pain and a high risk of a fall during an unassisted transfer attempt. Urgent after-hours assessment provided diagnostic validation, ruled out infection, and allowed for the immediate administration of targeted, higher-tier analgesia to break the pain-stiffness cycle and facilitate safe transfer to bed.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT to rapidly reduce the acute intra-articular inflammatory component of the flare. Prescribed a short 3-day course of Codeine 30mg PRN for severe breakthrough pain to facilitate sleep and morning mobilization.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Use a firm walking frame specifically when transitioning from sitting to standing over the next 48 hours. Consequence: The frame absorbs the immense mechanical load as the knee transitions from a bent to a straight position, preventing the sudden, agonizing "bone-on-bone" pain spike (negative reinforcement).
Action: Apply a hot, moist towel directly to the knee for 15 minutes before attempting to stand up after a period of rest. Consequence: The heat increases local blood flow and softens the stiffened joint capsule, drastically reducing the severe "gel phenomenon" and making the first steps much smoother (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral for physiotherapy and potential orthopedic surgical consultation (joint replacement assessment) via the regular GP.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided temporary use of a standard walking frame to ensure safe transfers.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to reassess baseline pain management and discuss long-term definitive options (e.g., total knee arthroplasty).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the knee suddenly balloons with fluid, becomes intensely hot and bright red, or if the patient develops a high fever with shivering (rigors).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis represents an acute flare of established degenerative joint disease. Formal weight-bearing X-rays are required to grade the severity of the arthritis. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This acute, severe pain spike will settle back to your baseline dull ache over 5-7 days with rest and medications. However, the underlying worn cartilage is permanent and will continue to flare if overloaded.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the knee suddenly becomes completely swollen, red, and hot to the touch, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The protective cartilage in your knee has worn away, causing the bones to grind together (the crunching sound). Standing for four hours today severely overloaded the joint, causing massive inflammation inside the capsule. The injected anti-inflammatory acts like a fire extinguisher to calm this acute flare.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to walk without the frame right now. The joint is highly unstable due to the pain. Use heat packs to melt the stiffness before you stand up.

